# Base Paper Limitation Agents

Multi-agent pipeline that reads a scientific paper and produces a synthesized, non-redundant,
numbered list of its limitations -- combining explicit author-stated limitations with inferred
methodological/theoretical/robustness/reproducibility/presentation issues.

Inspired by "Multi-Agent LLMs for Generating Research Limitations"
(https://arxiv.org/pdf/2601.11578), which uses many single-purpose agents plus a merger agent.
This implementation consolidates those into fewer specialist agents to cut latency/cost while
keeping the same analytical coverage, and lets the orchestrator do the final merge itself.

**`adk/`** -- an interactive Google ADK multi-agent app (`adk web` / `adk run`), driven by
file uploads.

---

## What the ADK pipeline does (`adk/`)

`adk/MasterAgent` is the root agent (`master_orchestrator_agent`). It currently wires in and
runs **2** specialist sub-agents, then synthesizes their outputs itself into the final
limitations list:

- **`content_limitations_agent`** ([adk/ContentLimitationsAgent](adk/ContentLimitationsAgent)) --
  combines explicit limitations (verbatim, author-stated), inferred limitations (critical
  methodological analysis), and open peer-review style limitations.
- **`technical_audit_agent`** ([adk/TechnicalAuditAgent](adk/TechnicalAuditAgent)) -- audits
  theory & assumptions, baselines/novelty/comparability, robustness/failure modes/ablations,
  reproducibility/compute/scalability, and presentation/clarity/impact.

Master (`master_orchestrator_agent`, in [adk/MasterAgent/agent.py](adk/MasterAgent/agent.py)):

1. Treats whatever artifact was uploaded as the base paper (uses the first artifact if
   multiple are uploaded -- no special filename required) and calls the
   `extract_main_paper_content` tool ([adk/MasterAgent/tools.py](adk/MasterAgent/tools.py)) to
   trim it down to just the main content -- through Conclusion, Limitations, and
   Ethical/Broader Impact Statement -- dropping References, Bibliography, Acknowledgments, and
   Appendix to save tokens/cost.
2. Passes only that trimmed text to `content_limitations_agent` and `technical_audit_agent`
   (never the raw artifact/file).
3. Collects both specialist outputs and merges them itself into one final, de-duplicated,
   numbered list -- no separate merger/critic agent call is needed.

**`citation_agent`** ([adk/CitationAgent](adk/CitationAgent)) is implemented and imported by
`MasterAgent/agent.py`, but is **not currently included** in `MasterAgent`'s `sub_agents` list,
so it isn't called as part of a normal run. It identifies limitations of the current paper's
own methodology grounded in evidence from papers it cites and papers that cite it, reading PDFs
from [adk/CitationAgent/citations](adk/CitationAgent/citations) via the
`extract_citation_key_sections` tool ([adk/CitationAgent/tools.py](adk/CitationAgent/tools.py)),
which trims each one down to just its Introduction and Limitations sections. It runs on a local
Ollama model (`qwen2.5:14b`) rather than Gemini. It can be run standalone (`adk run
CitationAgent`) or re-added to `MasterAgent`'s `sub_agents` list to bring it back into the main
pipeline.

Other agent folders under `adk/` (`ExtractorAgent`, `AnalyzerAgent`, `ReviewerAgent`,
`TheoryAssumptionsAgent`, `BaselinesNoveltyAgent`, `RobustnessFailureAgent`,
`ReproducibilityComputeAgent`, `PresentationClarityAgent`, `MergerAgent`, `GenericAgent`) are the
original, more granular single-purpose agents from the paper's design (plus a `MergerAgent` that
combined all of their outputs). They are legacy/unused by `MasterAgent` -- kept for reference,
safe to delete if not needed.

---

## Setup

```bash
# 1. Create and activate a virtual environment (from the repo root)
python3 -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate

# 2. Install dependencies
pip install -r requirements.txt

# 3. Configure your Gemini API key for the ADK app
#    (adk/.env is already git-ignored)
cat > adk/.env <<'EOF'
GOOGLE_GENAI_USE_VERTEXAI=0
GOOGLE_API_KEY=your-gemini-api-key-here
EOF
```

---

## Running the ADK web UI

```bash
source .venv/bin/activate
cd adk
adk web
```

- Open the URL printed in the terminal (default `http://localhost:8000`).
- In the top-left agent dropdown, select **`MasterAgent`**.
- In the chat panel, attach the paper you want analyzed (any filename works -- the first
  uploaded artifact is treated as the base paper).
- Send a message (e.g. "Analyze the limitations of this paper") and the Master agent will run
  the full pipeline and return the final numbered limitations list.

Alternative: run a single agent directly from the terminal instead of the web UI:

```bash
cd adk
adk run MasterAgent
```

To run `CitationAgent` on its own (requires a local Ollama server with `qwen2.5:14b` pulled,
and PDFs placed in [adk/CitationAgent/citations](adk/CitationAgent/citations)):

```bash
cd adk
adk run CitationAgent
```

---



## Security note

Never commit `adk/.env` (it holds your `GOOGLE_API_KEY`) -- it's already listed in
[.gitignore](.gitignore). If a key is ever exposed in git history or shared publicly, rotate it
immediately in Google AI Studio / Google Cloud Console.

